              model_answer:
                "Across the 300-year arc from 1450 to 1750, Great Britain underwent a breathtaking structural transformation, evolving from an impoverished, agrarian fringe on the edge of Eurasia into the world's preeminent commercial, naval, and constitutional superpower. However, evaluating whether this transformation created a truly 'modern' society reveals profound historical paradoxes: while Britain pioneered the institutional machinery of modernity, this machinery was built upon systemic inequality, domestic state terror, and the catastrophic exploitation of millions of enslaved Africans.<br><br>In economic and geopolitical terms, Britain’s metamorphosis was extraordinary. In 1450, England was economically devastated by the Hundred Years' War and the Black Death, producing no valuable manufactured exports and commercially choked by the Ottoman conquest of Constantinople. By 1750, the Royal Navy commanded 250 warships, protecting an oceanic trade empire that spanned the Atlantic and Indian Oceans (Source B). The Financial Revolution—marked by the founding of the Bank of England in 1694, the National Debt, and Lloyd’s marine insurance—provided unprecedented liquid capital that funded state expansion [2.1, 2.2]. In London, over 500 coffeehouses fostered the Consumer Revolution and an urban public sphere where literate citizens consumed daily newspapers and debated politics, embodying the civic vitality of a modern society [2.3].<br><br>Politically, Britain established the foundational principles of modern constitutional government. The Glorious Revolution of 1688 and the 1689 Bill of Rights (Source A) permanently destroyed the absolute divine right claimed by Charles I [1.1, 1.2]. By forbidding the Crown from suspending statutes, raising taxes, or maintaining a standing army without consent, Britain created the world's first constitutional monarchy, protecting individual property rights and rule of law. However, this constitutional breakthrough was not a modern democracy, but an aristocratic oligarchy [1.3]. Fewer than 3% of the population could vote, corruption flourished through rotten boroughs, and political authority remained firmly concentrated in the hands of wealthy landowners and merchant magnates.<br><br>Most decisively, the dark underbelly of 1750 shatters any simplistic claim to enlightened modernity. Domestically, rapid urbanization created horrific slum destitution in London, immortalized in William Hogarth’s 1751 masterpiece *Gin Lane* (Source C), where over 75% of infants died before age five [3.1, 3.2]. To protect their property from the impoverished masses, Parliament enacted the 'Bloody Code', expanding capital punishment to over 200 minor property offenses and staging gruesome public hangings at Tyburn [3.3]. Internationally, Britain's commercial ascendancy was directly subsidized by the calculated brutality of the Transatlantic Slave Trade, which commodified millions of African lives to fuel the sugar plantations of Jamaica and Barbados [2.2, 3.3].<br><br>Ultimately, traditional nineteenth-century 'Whig' historians who viewed British history as an inevitable march of enlightened progress ignored the brutal foundations of the imperial state. Britain in 1750 was indisputably modern in its financial institutions, constitutional limits, and global reach. Yet this modernity was fundamentally fractured—an empire whose commercial and constitutional genius was inextricably bound to colonial violence, human trafficking, and domestic class terror.",
